#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
We are given a simple polyline P0..P(n-1). It can be translated/rotated rigidly.
Question: can it pass through a point-hole at (0,0) and end up completely in y<0?

Key fact:
A rigid polyline can pass through a point-hole iff there exists a line through some point
of the polyline such that the whole polyline lies on one side of that line after placing
that point at the hole, and by continuous rotation/translation it can be moved through.
For a simple polyline, this is possible iff there exists a point on the polyline from which
the two incident "tails" fit into some open angle of size <= 180 degrees.
Equivalently, at some point of the polyline, the directions to all other points lie within
some semicircle.

For a polyline, it is enough to check vertices:
At an interior vertex i, let
  a = direction from Pi to P(i-1)
  b = direction from Pi to P(i+1)
The polyline can pass through vertex i iff the smaller angle between rays a and b is >= 180 deg,
i.e. the turn at the vertex is not "convex" toward both sides. In vector terms, this means
the two rays can be covered by some semicircle, which is always true unless they span > 180 deg.
For two rays this is equivalent to cross(a,b) <= 0 after taking the oriented angle from a to b
along the locally exterior side. More robustly, the condition becomes:
the angle between a and b is >= pi (or = pi).
Using dot/cross, angle >= pi only when they are opposite (pi), impossible for strict > pi
for two rays. So checking only vertices this way is insufficient.

Better characterization:
The polyline can pass through a point-hole iff it is "threadable", which for polygonal chains
is equivalent to existence of a point p on the chain where upper and lower convex hulls intersect
only at p while sweeping along the chain. A simpler O(n^2 log n) method acceptable for moderate n:
for every vertex i, consider all other vertices relative to Pi. If all directions fit in some
closed semicircle, answer YES. Also for a point in the interior of an edge, the set changes
continuously, and if feasible somewhere, feasible at some vertex for polygonal chain endpoints
of critical directions. This standard simplification works for this problem.

So for each vertex Pi:
- compute angles to every other vertex Pj (j != i)
- duplicate with +2pi
- sort
- if there exists an interval of length pi containing all n-1 directions, then YES.

This means max gap between consecutive angles is at least pi.
*/

static const long double PI = acosl(-1.0L);
static const long double EPS = 1e-14L;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;

    vector<long double> x(n), y(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        cin >> x[i] >> y[i];
    }

    if (n <= 2) {
        cout << "YES\n";
        return 0;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        vector<long double> ang;
        ang.reserve(n - 1);
        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
            if (i == j) continue;
            ang.push_back(atan2l(y[j] - y[i], x[j] - x[i]));
        }
        sort(ang.begin(), ang.end());
        int m = (int)ang.size();
        vector<long double> a(2 * m);
        for (int k = 0; k < m; ++k) {
            a[k] = ang[k];
            a[k + m] = ang[k] + 2 * PI;
        }

        int r = 0;
        for (int l = 0; l < m; ++l) {
            if (r < l) r = l;
            while (r + 1 < l + m && a[r + 1] - a[l] < PI + EPS) ++r;
            if (r - l + 1 >= m) {
                cout << "YES\n";
                return 0;
            }
        }
    }

    cout << "NO\n";
    return 0;
}
